 side with traces of a trackway ascending to it. Below the hillfort lie remains of a medieval house and enclosure. Now managed as a nature reserve by the Montgomeryshire Wildlife Trust since 1985, old lead and barites mines on the hill provide roosting habitat for bats.'' -> ''Roundton Hill is a steep-sided volcanic hill rising to 370 metres in eastern Montgomeryshire, Powys, Wales, near the English border at Church Stoke [1] [2]. Now managed as a nature reserve by the Montgomeryshire Wildlife Trust since 1985, old lead and barites mines on the hill provide roosting habitat for bats [1] [3].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245541", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245542", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384919", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384920", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
