lude geometric, zoomorphic and anthropomorphic images [1]. The Trialeti petroglyphs are located some 12 km east of the town of Tsalka, in the narrow gorge of the Avdris-Tsqali or Patara Khrami river—a right tributary of the Ktsia River—on the outskirt of the village of Gantiadi in the Kvemo Kartli region, in an area which was historically known as Trialeti [1]. This rock art site was discovered in the 1880s and rediscovered in 1976 [1]. The Trialeti petroglyphs include about 100 images, clustered into six panels, carved across 50 m of a flat basalt surface [1]. Animals are the most common depictions and include local fauna, such as deer, horses, mountain goats, birds, and fish, as well as fantastic and hybrid creatures [1]. Human figures are hunters, equipped with bows and arrows, and diminutive in comparison to the animals, measuring between 2.5 cm and 18–20 cm, mostly of 1–2 mm in width and depth [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92419", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92420", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:141981", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-001: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Tsalka_petroglyphs.png'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/0259ff6b/Trialeti_petroglyphs_(damaged),_May_2021.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245571", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245572", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-10000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:304631", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-001-001: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/0259ff6b/Trialeti_petrogl