    ('09895c88-e3d8-4ad2-b23e-eef85097ab6e'::uuid, 'empire_count', '1', '0', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:09895c88-e3d8-4ad2-b23e-eef85097ab6e:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: empire_count ''0'' restored on Bradley Hill Fort', 'c2f2b74c50f51ddb9ee03a048664e76d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 1 from tag_site(53.286613386104285, -2.6921818161632967, period_start None)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81728", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2041:chunk-0001: description ''Bradley Hill Fort is an Iron Age hillfort and the smallest of the seven hillforts in Cheshire, covering about 2 acres (8,100 sq m) with an enclosed area of roughly 1.5 acres. Located on the central ridge running north-south through the county, it is the furthest north of the Cheshire hillforts, positioned to exploit the steep banks of a tributary of the River Weaver for defence. Unlike other ridge forts commanding views of the Cheshire Plain, Bradley is surrounded on most sides by higher ground, restricting its views. It was defended by a single bank and ditch, 75 to 80 ft wide. It is protected as a Scheduled Ancient Monument.'' -> \"Bradley hill fort is an Iron Age hill fort [1]. Bradley lies on the central ridge that runs north–south through the county, as do all of the hill forts in Cheshire [1]. Bradley hill fort is surrounded on most sides by higher ground, so whereas other hill forts on the Cheshire central ridge command views of the Cheshire Plain, Bradley''s views are restricted [1]. A tributary of the River Weaver runs east–west past the north of the site, and the fort was positioned to exploit the stream''s steep banks for defence [1]. Covering about 2 acres (8,100 m2), with an enclosed area of about 1.5 acres (6,100 m2), Bradley is the smallest hill fort in Cheshire [1]. The site was defended by a single bank and ditch, 75 to 80 ft (23 to 24 m) wide from the back of the bank to the outer edge of the ditch [1]. The site has not been investigated archaeologically [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245649", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245650", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384958", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384959", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
