th of the site, and the fort was positioned to exploit the stream''s steep banks for defence [1]. Covering about 2 acres (8,100 m2), with an enclosed area of about 1.5 acres (6,100 m2), Bradley is the smallest hill fort in Cheshire [1]. The site was defended by a single bank and ditch, 75 to 80 ft (23 to 24 m) wide from the back of the bank to the outer edge of the ditch [1]. The site has not been investigated archaeologically [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245649", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245650", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384958", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384959", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
