ver Weaver runs east–west past the north of the site, and the fort was positioned to exploit the stream''s steep banks for defence [1]. Covering about 2 acres (8,100 m2), with an enclosed area of about 1.5 acres (6,100 m2), Bradley is the smallest hill fort in Cheshire [1]. The site was defended by a single bank and ditch, 75 to 80 ft (23 to 24 m) wide from the back of the bank to the outer edge of the ditch [1]. The site has not been investigated archaeologically [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92549", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245649", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245650", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384958", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384959", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('09fcbc60-19c1-4742-a69d-3cb7afe19443'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:09fcbc60-19c1-4742-a69d-3cb7afe19443:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: antiquity ''7'' restored on Kingston Russell Stone Circle', '7a25995a2b4d50ccb5f7be4dff79bc8a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33039", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0012:chunk-0001: description ''Kingston Russell Stone Circle, also known as the Gorwell Circle, is the largest surviving stone circle in Dorset, located on a chalk ridge between Abbotsbury and Littlebredy at 189 m above sea level. The late Neolithic or early Bronze Age monument consists of 18 sarsen stones arranged in an oval measuring 24 by 27 m. All stones are now recumbent; records from 1815 note one stone on the southern side was still upright at that time. The site has never been formally excavated. It is a scheduled monument under the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Areas Act 1979, managed by English Heritage.'' -> ''Kingston Russell Stone Circle, also known as the Gorwell Circle, is a stone circle located between the villages of Abbotsbury and Littlebredy in the south-western English county of Dorset [1]. Archaeologists believe that it was likely erected during the Bronze Age [1]. The Kingston Russell ring is part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, over a period between 3,300 and 900 BCE [1]. The site has not been excavated or been subject to in-depth archaeological investigation [1]. Kingston Russell is the largest surviving stone circle in Dorset [1]. The stones are arranged in an oval with a diameter measuring from 24 by 27 metres (79 by 89 feet) [1]. The ring consists of eighteen stones, the longest of which are located at the north of the circle [1]. As of 1939, all the stones were lying on the ground, although there is a record that in 1815 one stone on the southern side was still standing upright [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:92556", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:142685", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/09fcbc60/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/09fcbc60/Kingston Russell stone circle I.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245653", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:245654", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346288", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:346289", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s001: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384960", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384961", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_m