, whilst the smaller fort can be dated with reasonable certainty to around the 4th century BC [1]. Whilst the forts were constructed in different periods, archaeologists have uncovered evidence of concurrent occupation, seemingly up until around the 2nd century BC [1]. The larger fort contained around 50 roundhouses during its lifetime, whereas examinations of the smaller fort have turned up no more than six [1]. The fort is on the summit of a ridge of rhyolite at an altitude of approximately 800 feet and is enclosed by a single rampart [1]. The site has a commanding position overlooking Conwy Bay [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93304", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:93305", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s002: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:234573", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s001: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:234574", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s001: period_start None -> ''-600''"}]'::jsonb),
