    ('142c0275-1a5c-47dd-a562-c6874cafa728'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:142c0275-1a5c-47dd-a562-c6874cafa728:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Liddington Castle', 'e0189180643b86ad0f919024efa57b2f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33111", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0014:chunk-0001: description \"A Late Bronze Age and Early Iron Age univallate hillfort in Wiltshire, England, covering 3 hectares on a commanding hilltop close to the ancient Ridgeway trackway. Excavations by Hirst and Rahtz in 1976 revealed ramparts constructed in at least four phases beginning in the 7th-8th centuries BC, with initial timber-and-turf defences later enlarged to a massive dump rampart. Finds include a bronze awl, earring, spindle whorls, pottery, and a ritual shaft at least 2.4 metres deep. The fort was likely abandoned in the 5th century BC with some Roman-period reoccupation. It has been speculatively linked to Mons Badonicus, the legendary site of King Arthur''s victory over the Saxons.\" -> ''Liddington Castle, locally called Liddington Camp, is a late Bronze Age and early Iron Age univallate hillfort in Liddington parish in the English county of Wiltshire [1]. The site is on a commanding high point close to the Ridgeway and covers an area of 3 hectares [1]. Liddington Castle was one of the earliest hillforts in Britain, with first occupation dating to the 7th century BC [1]. The earthworks consist of a relatively simple oval bank of timber and earth fronted by a ditch, with opposing causewayed entrances on the east and west sides [1]. During a later phase the bank and ditch were improved and a rampart of dumped chalk increased the height of the bank [1]. The hillfort was partially excavated in 1976 [1]. The excavation revealed a large pit 1.5 metres in diameter and at least 2.4 metres deep [1]. The bottom of the feature was not reached and it was interpreted by the archaeologists who dug it as a ritual shaft [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307620", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/142c0275/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/142c0275/Ridgeway_10_Liddington.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
