 to the coast, it dominates the surrounding countryside [1]. The summit features a large and prominent archeological site; one of the largest hillforts in west Wales [1]. The lower slopes of Carningli are covered with traces of Bronze Age settlement and so some features of the hillfort may be even older [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35666", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-1000'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:113614", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s001: site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' -> ''Fort''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143445", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-003: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Mynydd_Carningli_-_geograph.org.uk_-_27994.jpg/400px-Mynydd_Carningli_-_geograph.org.uk_-_27994.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/163dc5dd/Mynydd Carningli - geograph.org.uk - 27994.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
