    ('18c1589b-3ebd-4267-8567-effd7e549792'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:18c1589b-3ebd-4267-8567-effd7e549792:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Bush Barrow', 'e9baf116c621d718150726a2dcee1823', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Barrow'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2051:chunk-0001: description ''Bush Barrow is an Early Bronze Age Wessex culture site at the western end of the Normanton Down Barrows cemetery in Wiltshire, England [1]. It is among the most important sites of the Stonehenge complex, having produced some of the most spectacular grave goods in Britain, including worked gold objects [1]. Excavated in 1808 by William Cunnington for Sir Richard Colt Hoare, the finds are displayed at Wiltshire Museum in Devizes [1].'' -> ''Bush Barrow is a site of the early British Bronze Age Wessex culture (c. 1950 BC), at the western end of the Normanton Down Barrows ancient cemetery in Wiltshire [1]. It is among the most important sites of the Stonehenge complex, having produced some of the most spectacular grave goods in Britain [1]. Bush Barrow lies around 1 kilometre southwest of Stonehenge on Normanton Down [1]. The barrow was excavated in 1808 by William Cunnington for Sir Richard Colt Hoare [1]. It contained a male skeleton, described as the \\''Bush Barrow chieftain\\'', with a collection of funerary goods that make it \"the richest and most significant example of a Bronze Age burial monument not only in the Normanton group or in association with Stonehenge, but arguably in the whole of Britain\" [1]. The items date the burial to the early Bronze Age, circa 1950 BC, and include a large \\''lozenge\\''-shaped sheet of gold, a sheet gold belt plate, three bronze daggers, a bronze axe, a stone macehead and bronze rivets, all held by the Wiltshire Museum, Devizes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:143887", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-004: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/18c1589b/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/18c1589b/Bush Barrow - geograph.org.uk - 1622275.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
