    ('255da98c-7cdf-46b3-878e-7eb55df2d8c2'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:255da98c-7cdf-46b3-878e-7eb55df2d8c2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Cave of Altamira', '7243db9a503e86c5f7706a53c8b2e9a8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82342", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2059:chunk-0001: description ''The Cave of Altamira is a cave complex near Santillana del Mar in Cantabria, Spain, renowned for its prehistoric cave art [1]. The cave features charcoal drawings and polychrome paintings depicting contemporary local fauna and human hands [1]. The earliest paintings date to the Upper Paleolithic period, approximately 36,000 years ago [1]. The site was discovered in 1868 by Modesto Cubillas and later studied by Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola [1].'' -> \"The Cave of Altamira is a cave complex, located near the historic town of Santillana del Mar in Cantabria [1]. It is renowned for prehistoric cave art featuring charcoal drawings and polychrome paintings of contemporary local fauna and human hands [1]. The site was discovered in 1868 by Modesto Cubillas and subsequently studied by Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola [1]. Aside from the striking quality of its multicolor art, Altamira''s fame stems from the fact that its paintings were the first European cave paintings for which a prehistoric origin was suggested and promoted [1]. Altamira is located in the Franco-Cantabrian region and in 1985 was declared a World Heritage Site by UNESCO as a key location of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain [1]. The cave is approximately 270 m long and consists of a series of twisting passages and chambers [1]. In 2008, researchers using uranium-thorium dating found that the paintings were completed over a period of up to 20,000 years rather than during a comparatively brief period [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:144704", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/255da98c/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/255da98c/Cave_of_Altamira_and_Paleolithic_Cave_Art_of_Northern_Spain-110113.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308546", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/255da98c/Cave_of_Altamira_and_Paleolithic_Cave_Art_of_Northern_Spain-110113.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/255da98c/9_Bisonte_Magdaleniense_polícromo.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
