oric World Heritage Site[1].\" -> \"The Rock Art of Alta consists of over 6,000 prehistoric carvings in northern Norway''s Finnmark county [1]. The largest concentration at Jiepmaluokta, about 5 km from Alta, features thousands of individual carvings [1]. Five sites including Jiepmaluokta, Storsteines, Kåfjord, Amtmannsnes, and Transfarelv were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List on December 3, 1985, making this Norway''s only prehistoric World Heritage Site [1] [2].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235645", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235646", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''-5000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308612", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/280e2bbb/20171102ingvild007.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/280e2bbb/Alta-Carvings-2012-07-17-12-15-018.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2851ba0a-1403-47b9-a605-deb3735046ca'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:2851ba0a-1403-47b9-a605-deb3735046ca:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Caer Bach', '1e1c3a1dfc74dffad3cfd7ebdc4038f2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:82432", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2061:chunk-0001: description \"Caer Bach is a small hillfort in Conwy County Borough, North Wales, on the eastern slopes of Tal-y-Fan, an outlying peak of the Carneddau mountain range in northeast Snowdonia[1]. The name translates from Welsh as ''Small Fort'' or ''Little Fortress''[1]. Two lines of defence circle the rounded hillock[2]. The site remains of indeterminate age with minimal investigation conducted to date[1]. The location sits at approximately 413 metres elevation, offering strategic views over the surrounding landscape[1].\" -> \"Caer Bach is a Welsh hillfort of indeterminate age, on a rounded hillock situated near the village of Rowen in Conwy County Borough, North Wales [1]. Its name in English translates as ''Small Fort''; or alternatively ''Little Fortress'' [1]. It is situated on the Eastern slopes of Tal-y-Fan, an outlying peak of the Carneddau mountain range in northeast Snowdonia; at an altitude of approximately 413 metres [1]. Little information is known about the history of the fort, as to date, the site has undergone minimal investigation [1]. The fort is classed as a scheduled ancient monument and is of national importance for its potential to enhance our knowledge of prehistoric settlement [1]. The site has been identified as a partial contour fort, unusual for its poorly defensible position within the wider landscape, and also for its small size [1]. Thirty yards southwest of the fort are the remains of a round hut, about 24 feet in diameter and cut into the slope on the northeast and dating from the Iron Age / Roman period [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95353", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:95354", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s004: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145370", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2851ba0a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2851ba0a/Caer Bach (left) - a Celtic hillfort of indeterminate age, on a rounded hillock situated near the village of Rowen; in Conwy County, Wales 13.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246948", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308624", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2851ba0a/Caer Bach (left) - a Celtic hillfort of indeterminate age, on a rounded hillock situated near the village of Rowen; in Conwy County, Wales 13.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2851ba0a/Remains_of_Stone_Wall_at_Caer_Bach.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347237", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated'