    ('28d08d27-d959-42d0-922c-8e91c795d432'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:28d08d27-d959-42d0-922c-8e91c795d432:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Griffin Warrior Tomb', 'edb77eddc07b740aefb0e12611deb283', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Tomb'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 10 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28412", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0128:chunk-0001: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33467", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''The Griffin Warrior Tomb is a Bronze Age shaft grave dating to c. 1450 BC, discovered in 2015 by University of Cincinnati archaeologists Jack Davis and Sharon Stocker near the Palace of Nestor in Pylos, Greece. The undisturbed burial yielded over 2,000 artifacts including gold jewelry, bronze weapons, ivory plaques, and approximately 50 decorated seal stones. Among them, the Pylos Combat Agate -- a 1.4-inch sealstone depicting warriors in extraordinary detail -- is considered one of the finest works of prehistoric Greek art. The predominantly Minoan-style grave goods suggest deep cultural exchange between Minoan and Mycenaean civilizations rather than sharp division.'' -> ''The Griffin Warrior Tomb is a Bronze Age shaft tomb dating to around 1450 BC, near the ancient city of Pylos in Greece [1]. The grave was discovered by a research team sponsored by the University of Cincinnati and led by husband-and-wife archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon Stocker [1]. The tomb site was excavated from May to October 2015 [1]. During the initial six month excavation, the research team uncovered an intact adult male skeleton and excavated 1400 objects including weapons, jewels, armour and silver and gold artifacts [1]. Since 2015, the number of artifacts recovered from the grave has reached over 3500 items, including a historically significant Minoan sealstone called the Pylos Combat Agate and four signet gold rings with detailed images from Minoan mythology [1]. The gravesite was discovered in an olive grove near the ancient Palace of Nestor, within the Bronze Age city of Pylos, in southwest Greece [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
