enclosed within a rectangular perimeter wall -- an unusual design among Roman cities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today.'' -> ''The Roman Theatre of Aosta (Teatro Romano) is an ancient theatre in the city of Aosta in the Aosta Valley, northern Italy, built during the Julio-Claudian dynasty [1] [2] [3]. Its imposing 22-metre-high facade wall remains towering over the surrounding urban area [1] [4]. The semicircular cavea and stage were enclosed within a rectangular perimeter wall -- an unusual design among Roman cities, positioned near the Porta Praetoria to facilitate crowd movement [4] [5]. Substantial restoration was carried out in the 1930s under archaeologist Pietro Barocelli, and the theatre continues to host performances today [4] [6] [7].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246965", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246966", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''43''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347256", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:347257", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s002: period_start ''43'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2adae6b7-2156-4080-aaa0-921dd7f8a9c9'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:2adae6b7-2156-4080-aaa0-921dd7f8a9c9:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: antiquity ''8'' restored on Carreg Samson', 'b93d77643edb3f9edcd600547315913a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33503", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''Carreg Samson is a 5,000-year-old Neolithic dolmen overlooking Abercastle Harbour on the Pembrokeshire coast of Wales. The massive capstone, measuring between 15 and 18 feet long according to early 19th-century surveyor Richard Fenton, rests on three of six large upright stones, though Fenton recorded four supports in 1810, indicating structural shifting over the centuries. Excavated by Frances Lynch in August 1968, the site yielded a pottery vessel containing the cremated ashes of several individuals buried beneath a floor of yellow clay. Named after Saint Samson of Dol, who according to legend placed the capstone with his little finger, the monument is part of approximately 80 dolmens in northern Pembrokeshire reflecting Neolithic maritime cultural exchange along the Irish Sea.'' -> ''Carreg Samson is a 5000-year-old Neolithic dolmen located half a mile west of Abercastle near the Pembrokeshire Coast Path in Wales [1]. Carreg Samson has a capstone, 4.7 metres by 2.7 metres and 1.0 metre thick [1]. The capstone rests on three of six stones 1.1 to 2.2 metres high [1]. The whole burial chamber was once covered by a mound of earth or stones and once these were removed stones were used to block the holes in the sides of the tomb so that it could be used as a shelter for sheep [1]. The site was excavated in 1968 which revealed four additional stone-holes, one having supported a further chamber stone, the others indicating a possible passage leading off to the northwest [1]. Slight traces of a covering cairn were found to the south and it was shown that the monument had been raised over a pit 0.8 metres deep, filled with clay and stones [1]. Finds included a small quantity of burnt bone, pottery, and flints [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96295", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:96296", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s005: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145482", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2adae6b7/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2adae6b7/Carreg_Samson,_Sir_Benfro_in_2021_13.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246969", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:246970", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s004: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:308674", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/2adae6b7/Carreg_Samson,_Sir_Benfro_in_2021_13.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2adae6b7/Carreg_Samson,_Sir_Benfro_in_2021_08.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385058", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385059", "url"