es that illuminate the socio-economic context behind megalithic site placement in prehistoric Corsica.'' -> ''Pughjaredda (also spelled Poghjaredda) is a megalithic archaeological site in the commune of Sotta, southern Corsica, dating to the Middle Neolithic period (ca. 5000-4000 BC) [1] [2] [3] [4]. The site features dolmens, menhirs, and stone circles, along with evidence of domestic dwelling and funerary activities [2]. Studies at Pughjaredda have provided key insights into Corsican-Sardinian megalithism [2].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: lat ''41.5457001069429'' -> ''41.570833''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235688", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: lon ''9.195523410159458'' -> ''9.199167''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2b7bb7a9-4759-41bf-a703-8f4da42a1b5c'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:2b7bb7a9-4759-41bf-a703-8f4da42a1b5c:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Toquepala Caves', '32241dae37fd058d58441550dab581ae', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Rock art'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33507", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''Prehistoric caves located 26 km southeast of Moquegua and near the Toquepala mine in southeastern Peru, at an elevation of 2,300 metres. The caves contain over 50 rock paintings dating to approximately 7650 BC, depicting hunting scenes where armed figures corral and kill guanacos using axes, lances, and spear throwers. The art was painted in red, yellow, white, and black pigments made from hematite. Discovered by a mine worker in the 1950s, the caves were formally studied by Jorge C. Muelle and Rogger Ravines between 1963 and 1967.'' -> ''Toquepala Caves are located near Toquepala mine, about 154 km from the city of Tacna, in the extreme southeast of Peru [1]. They are notable for a number of rock paintings [1]. The best known of them is the cave named Abrigo del Diablo [1]. The caves were seasonally occupied [1]. The caves have rock art of gatherer-hunters who are inferred to have lived in the caves about 10,000 years ago [1]. There are more than 50 rock paintings drawn by the inhabitants of the caves of the Palaeolithic age of which 30 were studied [1]. These scenes depict armed people hunting the guanaco and Andean religious symbols [1]. The scene depicted is of hunting by the humans who are corralling and killing a group of guanacos [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:145510", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-006: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Toquepala_Caves_1.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/2b7bb7a9/Toquepala_Caves_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
