e Moor in the North York Moors, along the route of the Lyke Wake Walk [1]. Though now used as a parish boundary marker, it is believed to date from pre-Roman times [1]. The stone is heavily weathered and bears an engraved ''E 1841'' date on its south face [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247439", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247440", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s005: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348086", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:348087", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Longstone of Minchinhampton', 'a312ff3c82420945c6a01552f876947d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0023:chunk-0001: description ''A late Neolithic or early Bronze Age standing stone on Minchinhampton Common in Gloucestershire, standing 2.3 m high and made of oolitic limestone. The stone is perforated by two large and several smaller natural holes caused by weathering, and is believed to be the closing slab from a destroyed burial chamber or portal dolmen. Local folklore held that the holes had curative powers, and children with whooping cough or rickets were passed through them. A fallen stone 11 m to the southwest, now built into a dry stone wall, may have formed a pair with the Longstone. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument.'' -> ''The Longstone of Minchinhampton is a standing stone on Minchinhampton Common, Minchinhampton in Gloucestershire, England [1]. The Longstone is a large piece of oolitic limestone standing upright in a field, known as Longstone Field, 1 mile east of the Minchinhampton village [1]. It is 7.5 feet high and 6 feet wide, with natural holes through its 15 inches width [1]. Nearby, 12 yards to the south west, there is a fallen standing stone which has been built into a dry stone wall, and may have once formed a stone pair with the Longstone [1]. The area has numerous barrows and near the Longstone ornaments, flint and arrowheads have all been found [1]. The stone was believed to be able to cure infants of maladies such as measles and whooping cough, by passing the child through one of the natural holes [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97227", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:97228", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s006: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247781", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247782", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357141", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:357142", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-25p_fields-wd5-s003: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-1600''"}]'::jsonb),
