    ('43e8118f-ea04-4525-88a6-ed50a41bd240'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:43e8118f-ea04-4525-88a6-ed50a41bd240:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Puente Romano, Mérida', '9f64a5d17ba5f5733f12143fd910ddde', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34379", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0026:chunk-0001: description \"The Puente Romano is a Roman bridge over the Guadiana River at Mérida, southwest Spain, and the world''s longest surviving bridge from antiquity. Originally built in the 1st century BC when Emerita Augusta was founded, it once spanned 755 metres with 62 arches. Today 60 spans remain across 721 metres, with three buried on the southern bank. The granite and Roman concrete structure underwent restorations by the Visigoth Sala in 686 and Philip II in 1610. Part of the UNESCO World Heritage ensemble of Mérida, it was pedestrianized in 1991.\" -> ''The Puente Romano is a Roman bridge over the Guadiana River at Mérida in southwest Spain [1]. The piers are designed to withstand river current as they are rounded on the upstream side and square on the downstream side [1]. Puente Romano has undergone at least two significant restorations: once by Sala, a Visigoth, in 686 and once by Phillip II in 1610 [1]. Today, there are 60 spans on a length of 721 m between the abutments [1]. Including the approaches, the structure totals 790 m [1].''"}, {"sourc