    ('449c3f0a-6666-4355-8dbf-db881bd8866a'::uuid, 'empire_count', '1', '0', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:449c3f0a-6666-4355-8dbf-db881bd8866a:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: empire_count ''0'' restored on Caer Gwrtheyrn', '48cd967e248652accf72d705ce68868e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 1 from tag_site(52.03851146275636, -4.285267073911153, period_start None)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:83428", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2080:chunk-0001: description ''Caer Gwrtheyrn (Craig Gwrtheyrn) is an Iron Age hillfort on a prominent hill overlooking the River Teifi, located about 1.9 km west of Llanfihangel-ar-Arth in Carmarthenshire, Wales[1]. The fortifications measure approximately 160 m north-south and 125 m east-west, enclosing roughly 1.5 hectares (3.7 acres)[1]. The drystone rampart represents typical Iron Age defensive architecture in Wales[1].'' -> ''Craig Gwrtheyrn, or Caer Gwrtheyrn, is an Iron Age hillfort on the south side of the River Teifi, about 1.2 miles (1.9 km) west of the village of Llanfihangel-ar-Arth, in Carmarthenshire, Wales [1]. The fort is on an imposing hill overlooking the River Teifi [1]. The defences measure about 160 metres north to south, and 125 metres east to west, following the contours of the hill, enclosing an area of about 1.5 hectares [1]. The drystone rampart, of which traces remain, was originally 2–3 metres thick [1]. There is a ditch around the rampart (except on the very steep north-east side); stones from the rampart have fallen into this [1]. There is an entrance on the south-west, where two further banks create a barbican and where there is also a small area of chevaux de frise: sharp stones set on end, of which about 30 remain [1]. Craig Gwrtheyrn, or Caer Gwrtheyrn, is associated with the 5th-century British warlord Vortigern [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98037", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:98038", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s007: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247885", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:247886", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385729", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385730", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
