as part of the Stonehenge Riverside Project [1]. The stone was originally a natural feature, which sometime before 2000 BC, was placed in an upright position [1]. Around 2000 BC the Cuckoo Stone became the focus for several nearby cremation burials [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:148800", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4c648b74/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4c648b74/Cuckoo Stone Near Woodhenge, Durrington June 2015. - panoramio.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:310433", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-005: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/4c648b74/Cuckoo Stone Near Woodhenge, Durrington June 2015. - panoramio.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/4c648b74/Cuckoo_Stone_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
