e of pottery now known as Ballyalton bowls [1].'' -> ''Ballyalton Court Cairn is a Neolithic court tomb situated 0.8 km from Ballyalton village, approximately 3.6 km east of Downpatrick, County Down, Northern Ireland [1] [2] [3]. It is one of approximately 400 court tombs found primarily in northern Ireland, representing a distinctive class of megalithic chambered graves [3] [4] [5]. Archaeological excavations revealed human bones, flint implements, and a distinctive style of pottery now known as Ballyalton bowls [2] [4] [6].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:343754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-010-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/50f3f5c5/Dolmen at Ballyalton - geograph.org.uk - 2527862.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385752", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385753", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('50f9ccc8-06b1-4283-af88-a6769d0b9083'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:50f9ccc8-06b1-4283-af88-a6769d0b9083:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: antiquity ''7'' restored on Nine Stones, Winterbourne Abbas', 'd5d09549f4eb30f54971aa89b25fc811', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28224", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0097:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3300''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34493", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0029:chunk-0001: description ''A late Neolithic to early Bronze Age stone circle near Winterbourne Abbas in Dorset, dating to approximately 2000 BC. The circle has a maximum diameter of about 8 metres and consists of nine irregularly shaped stones ranging from 0.5 to 1.5 metres in height. Two notably larger stones on the northwest side, separated by a 3-metre gap suggesting a ceremonial entrance, distinguish the monument. Its valley-bottom location is described as very unusual for such monuments, and it is one of only four surviving stone circles in south Dorset. Managed by English Heritage.'' -> \"The Nine Stones, also known as the Devil''s Nine Stones, the Nine Ladies, or Lady Williams and her Dog, is a stone circle located near to the village of Winterbourne Abbas in the southwestern English county of Dorset [1]. The Nine Stones is part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread through much of Great Britain, Ireland, and Brittany between 3,300 and 900 BCE, during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age [1]. The Nine Stones circle is positioned at the national grid reference 36100904, on the western edge of the village of Winterbourne Abbas in Dorset [1]. The roots of a beech tree have engulfed two of the megaliths in the circle [1]. Seven of the nine surviving stones are under 90 centimetres tall, but two of the northwestern stones are considerably larger [1]. The circle is located at the bottom of a narrow valley [1]. In the nineteenth century the site was visited by the antiquarian Charles Warne, who wrote about it in his 1872 book Ancient Dorset [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35766", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3300'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84222", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:84223", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26a_fields-wd1-s001: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:149521", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-011: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/The_Winterbourne_Abbas_Nine_Stones_-_geograph.org.uk_-_25044.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/50f9ccc8/Nine_Stones,_Dorset.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302122", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385755", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_me