k construction ramps are still visible inside the great court. Despite its incomplete state, it serves as the main entrance to the Karnak temple complex today.'' -> ''The First Pylon of Karnak is the monumental entrance to the Precinct of Amun-Re, built by Nectanebo I during the 30th Dynasty (380-362 BC) but never completed [1] [2] [3]. It measures 113 metres wide and 15 metres thick, with the south tower reaching approximately 31.65 metres and the north tower about 21.70 metres, against a projected height of 38-40 metres [1] [2]. The mud brick construction ramps are still visible inside the great court [1] [2]. Despite its incomplete state, it serves as the main entrance to the Karnak temple complex today [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:355862", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-24_mechanical-site-type-merge-s002: site_type ''Gate'' -> ''Gate/archway/bridge''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5e1bd6c1-43bd-4a34-a7ae-7ea240f9f7b2'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:5e1bd6c1-43bd-4a34-a7ae-7ea240f9f7b2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on La Chapelle-aux-Saints', 'f11d8f25f65deb237f2b92e6f6d32b1a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Cave Structures'', ''Undated'') is shared by 52 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:80910", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2012:chunk-0001: description ''A cave site in Correze, central France, where the first recognized Neanderthal burial was discovered on 3 August 1908 by brothers Jean and Amedee Bouyssonie. The adult male skeleton, dating to roughly 50,000 years ago, was intentionally buried in a pit in the cave floor, providing the earliest evidence of mortuary ritual among Neanderthals. The individual died in his 30s despite surviving severe osteoarthritis, tooth loss, and a healed rib fracture, suggesting a social network that supported injured group members. Over 1,000 Mousterian flint tools and fauna including reindeer, horse, and rhinoceros remains were also recovered.'' -> ''The La Chapelle-aux-Saints cave, bordering the Sourdoire valley, revealed many archeological artifacts belonging to the late Mousterian techno-complex, including the first ever recognized Neanderthal burial discovered on 3 August 1908 [1]. The Bouffia Bonneval is the site of the discovery in 1908 of the Man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, a site listed as a historical monument by decree of March 11, 1981 [1].''"}, {"source