n diameter; the satellites average about 15 meters [1]. By the end of the 19th century the tomb had been investigated by antiquarians of the period who recorded finding ''bones of horses'', charred wood and a worked flint javelin head [1]. In the late 1990s, Goran Burenhult, a Swedish archaeologist, partly excavated Listoghil [1]. The chamber at the heart of the mound is a dolmen-like structure, with 6 orthostats [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:151761", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/County Sligo - Carrowmore Passage Tomb - 20180708152404.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:312117", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-007: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/County Sligo - Carrowmore Passage Tomb - 20180708152404.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/68181f11/Central_Chamber.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
