rl ''/data/images/wiki/6b926b71/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/6b926b71/Cleeve Hill.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338017", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4319:chunk-0001: description ''Cleeve Hill, the highest point of the Cotswolds at 330 metres, hosts a small multivallate Iron Age hillfort dating from c. 500 BC, one of 35 hillforts along the Cotswold escarpment. Originally a single bank and ditch, a second ring was added two centuries later, with ramparts up to 9.1m wide and 2.5m high. A Bronze Age cross dyke predating the hillfort served as a territorial boundary that still marks modern parish lines. The Ring, a circular Romano-British enclosure of c. 50 metres diameter, was likely used for livestock. Human activity on Cleeve Common spans approximately 6,000 years.'' -> ''Cleeve Hill, the highest point of the Cotswolds at 330 metres, hosts a small multivallate Iron Age hillfort dating from c. 500 BC [1] [2] [3] [4]. Human activity on Cleeve Common spans approximately 6,000 years [4].''"}]'::jsonb),
