millennia BC to the Roman invasion [1]. The fort is situated on a south facing promontory at the end of a 240m high ridge in the Blackdown Hills [1]. Originally a Neolithic site, an Iron Age hill fort was later built on the same site [1]. It was excavated between 1930 and 1935 by Dorothy Liddell [1]. She identified a timber framed entrance to the causewayed enclosure and an oval arrangement of postholes in the middle which she interpreted as being a building destroyed by fire before the enclosure earthworks were built [1]. During an excavation in the 1980s headed by Malcolm Todd, archaeological evidence was found on the site of Roman military occupation, suggesting a fort within the existing Iron Age site [1]. The site has given its name to some of the earliest Neolithic pottery in southern Britain after a large collection of pieces of this type of pottery were found during excavations by Dorothy Liddell [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101798", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:101799", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s011: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153171", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Hembury Hill III.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250123", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250124", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Hembury Hill III.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/786c39f1/Payhembury, Hembury hillfort - geograph.org.uk - 47183.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:385889", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s002: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('788f9236-c12f-4f94-9dc6-4f3a3d682626'::uuid, 'mystery', '9', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:788f9236-c12f-4f94-9dc6-4f3a3d682626:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''8'' restored on Santuário Rupestre de Panóias', 'c3871d27ffd6b7682e63019866cfc72f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 9 from (''Sanctuary'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 2 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85462", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2114:chunk-0001: description ''The Sanctuary of Panoias is a Gallo-Roman rock sanctuary near Vila Real in northern Portugal, dating to the late 2nd to early 3rd century AD. It was dedicated to the infernal god Serapis and local Lapitean deities by the Roman senator Gaius Calpurnius Rufinus, likely from Perge in Asia Minor. Three large granite outcrops contain carved tanks (lacus), roasting cavities (gastra), and purification basins used in elaborate initiation rites involving animal sacrifice, blood collection, viscera burning, and ritual consumption. Four surviving inscriptions in Latin and Greek describe the ceremonies and identify the dedicant.'' -> ''The Panóias Sanctuary, also called \"Fragas de Panóias\", is located in Vale de Nogueiras, in the municipality of Vila Real, Portugal [1]. Its construction dates back to the end of the 2nd century - early 3rd century AD [1]. The sanctuary is an enclosure with three large rocks, where several cavities of various sizes were opened and access stairs were also built [1]. On the rock located at the entrance of the enclosure, several inscriptions were engraved - three in Latin and one in Greek, describing the ritual celebrated [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:119045", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s005: site_type ''Cave Structures'' -> ''Sanctuary''"}]'::jsonb),
