 Llangedwyn, in Powys [1]. The hillfort has a single rampart, about 264 metres north to south and 204 metres east to west, following the contours of a prominent hill [1]. The area is about 3 hectares [1]. There is a simple inturned entrance on the south-east [1]. There was excavation in 1983 [1]. It was found that the site was first occupied in the late Bronze Age, about the 10th–9th century BC; the defences were enlarged about the 4th–3rd century BC, during the Iron Age [1]. Built into the rear of the rampart was an Iron Age smithy, for bronze-casting and iron-working, probably to serve the requirements of the settlement [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:153728", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-017: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/7a0bddc9/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/7a0bddc9/Llwyn Bryn-Dinas 1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
