    ('80ea9e97-d2ed-46f6-b1db-293dfd277039'::uuid, 'empire_count', '1', '0', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:80ea9e97-d2ed-46f6-b1db-293dfd277039:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: empire_count ''0'' restored on Grimsbury Castle', '4906647ba559be4795805dea73b695ef', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 1 from tag_site(51.447373524093024, -1.2659785451108727, period_start None)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85656", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2120:chunk-0001: description ''Grimsbury Castle is a slight univallate Iron Age hill fort situated within Grimsbury Wood between Cold Ash and Hermitage in Berkshire, enclosing about 8 acres of plateau. The defenses consist of a single bank and ditch with a combined height generally exceeding 2m, with two entrances featuring associated outworks. A natural spring within the enclosure has reportedly never run dry, and the rampart was extended southward to enclose it. Partial excavation by P. Wood in 1958-1960 yielded only three sherds of Early Iron Age pottery and a greensand quern stone fragment, leaving extensive unexcavated archaeological potential. Slight univallate hillforts are rare, with only about 150 examples surviving nationally.'' -> ''Grimsbury Castle is an Iron Age \"multiple enclosure\" Hill Fort comprising a large circular encampment on a high hill [1]. It is within Grimsbury Wood, between Cold Ash and Hermitage, in the county of Berkshire [1]. The site benefits from a natural spring which reportedly has never been known to run dry [1]. The entrenchment would appear to have been extended on the south side of the hill for the purpose of enclosing this spring [1]. This rampart appears to have had only two entrances, one on the north and the other on the south side; just within the entrenchment, at the entrance on the north, is a small tumulus, which may have been constructed as a mount for observation or defense, or for the purpose of interment [1]. The name shows that the later Saxon settlers in the region found the earthworks so impressive that they thought they must have been built by the chief of their gods, Woden alias Grim [1]. The site lies at an elevation of 155m AOD [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102582", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:102583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s012: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250203", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250204", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s011: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313158", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-008: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/80ea9e97/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/80ea9e97/Grimsbury Castle, Iron Age Hill Fort - geograph.org.uk - 886.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386515", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386516", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
