    ('882c38f5-5b59-47b8-b6df-27f6d07b7abe'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:882c38f5-5b59-47b8-b6df-27f6d07b7abe:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Eleutherna Bridge', '7333a2082f646a3ebc9da34d3fc83e0d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85826", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2124:chunk-0001: description ''The Eleutherna Bridge is a Hellenistic corbel arch bridge near the ancient city of Eleutherna in Crete, Greece, built in the 2nd century BC to cross the Halopotas river. Measuring 9.5 meters long and 5.25 meters wide, it uses an ecphoric (corbelling) technique where each unmortared limestone slab protrudes slightly from the one below, forming a triangular opening with a span of nearly 4 meters. Part of the bridge is carved directly into the bedrock. A similar second bridge nearby collapsed in the late 19th century. It remains preserved in excellent condition in a green ravine with cypresses and plane trees.'' -> ''The Eleutherna Bridge is an ancient Greek corbel arch bridge near the Cretan town of Eleutherna, Greece [1]. The well-preserved structure has a single span of 3.95 m, which is quite large for a false arch [1]. The opening is cut from the unmortared limestone blocks in the shape of an isosceles triangle, the height of which is 1.84 m [1]. The overall length of the bridge measures 9.35 m [1]. Its width varies from 5.1 to 5.2 m [1]. The height is between 4 and 4.2 m [1]. The bridge, which is still in use, was first described by the Englishman T.A.B. Spratt in his Travels and Researches in Crete, after he had paid a visit to the site in 1853 [1]. According to Nakassis, the extant, northern bridge was built sometime during the Hellenistic period, while the Italian scholar Galliazzo dates the construction more precisely to the end of the 4th or beginning of the 3rd century BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:154747", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-018: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/Eleutherna Bridge, Crete, Greece. Pic 04.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334568", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/Eleutherna Bridge, Crete, Greece. Pic 04.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
