4 and 4.2 m [1]. The bridge, which is still in use, was first described by the Englishman T.A.B. Spratt in his Travels and Researches in Crete, after he had paid a visit to the site in 1853 [1]. According to Nakassis, the extant, northern bridge was built sometime during the Hellenistic period, while the Italian scholar Galliazzo dates the construction more precisely to the end of the 4th or beginning of the 3rd century BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:154747", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-018: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/Eleutherna Bridge, Crete, Greece. Pic 04.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334568", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/Eleutherna Bridge, Crete, Greece. Pic 04.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/882c38f5/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
