earby Roman settlers. Norse settlers named the valley \"Borgar dalr\" (valley of the fortress), referencing the ancient fort. An 18th-century slate quarry destroyed much of the southern fortification. The summit was given to the National Trust as a World War One memorial to Borrowdale\\''s fallen.'' -> ''Castle Crag is a hill in the North Western Fells of the English Lake District [1]. The wooded height of Castle Crag rises between Broadslack Gill and the Derwent [1]. The slopes of Castle Crag are extensively quarried with pits and levels on the northern and south eastern flanks [1]. The summit has also been extensively worked [1]. The summit area is believed to have been an ancient hill fort, although the western section has been sliced away by quarrying [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155336", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8baf0d5a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8baf0d5a/Castle_crag.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250669", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250670", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313922", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8baf0d5a/Castle_crag.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8baf0d5a/Castle_Crag_summit.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350842", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:350843", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s006: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8c8043b6-5c87-4366-9f84-acf61dee4294'::uuid, 'mystery', '2', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:8c8043b6-5c87-4366-9f84-acf61dee4294:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Castle Knowe, Northumberland', 'df4e9cebca0e241c13ac8f79a529a41c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 2 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''Undated'') is shared by 187 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85904", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2127:chunk-0001: description ''Castle Knowe, also known as Clinch Castle, is a multivallate Iron Age hillfort in the Cheviot Hills of Northumberland, about 1.6 km south-east of Ingram. The fort summit measures approximately 75 metres by 60 metres, defended by three roughly concentric ramparts formed by scarping the natural slope, standing up to 2.8 metres externally. The outer and middle ramparts merge on the steep western side. An original entrance on the east side features slightly inturned rampart ends. Remains of seven or more roundhouses survive within the enclosure. The fort is part of a dense concentration of Iron Age settlement in the Breamish Valley, with contemporary hillforts visible on Old Fawden Hill and Chubden Hill. It is a scheduled monument.'' -> \"Castle Knowe, also known as Clinch Castle, is the site of an Iron Age hillfort in Northumberland, England, about 1 mile south-east of the village of Ingram [1]. It is a scheduled monument [1]. The fort is in the Cheviot Hills, where there are other Iron Age sites including those at Old Fawden Hill and Chubden Hill, visible from Castle Knowe to the south-west [1]. The hill has steep slopes to the west and north, less steep to the east and south [1]. The fort on the summit measures about 75 metres west to east and 60 metres north to south; there are three roughly concentric ramparts on the slopes of the hill, of which the outer rampart and middle ramparts merge on the west side [1]. The fort''s outer rampart is the most substantial [1]. There is an original entrance on the east side, where the rampart''s ends are slightly inturned [1]. There are remains of seven or more roundhouses within the fort, of diameter 3.8 to 8.4 metres; there is some overlapping, indicating that there was more than one phase of occupation [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103413", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103414", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250677", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:250678", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s012: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386553", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386554", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
