"2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8ece5123-5b53-4545-929d-0519137c13ea'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:8ece5123-5b53-4545-929d-0519137c13ea:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Rhodes Footbridge', 'f01c6167e463b0a0e9c1461f9977b642', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:85964", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2129:chunk-0001: description ''The Rhodes Footbridge is an ancient Greek arch bridge in the city of Rhodes, dating to the 4th century BC or early Hellenistic period. It represents the oldest known Greek bridge constructed with a voussoir arch, where wedge-shaped stones distribute weight to maintain structural integrity. The bridge was unearthed in 1966-67 near the eastern harbour at Akandia Bay, where an artificial channel ran parallel to the ancient city walls. Its discovery demonstrates early Greek mastery of arch construction techniques that predated the more celebrated Roman arch bridges.'' -> ''The Rhodes Footbridge is an ancient Greek arch bridge in the city of Rhodes, Greece [1]. Dating to the 4th century BC or early Hellenistic period, the modest structure represents the oldest known Greek bridge with a voussoir arch [1]. The Rhodes Footbridge was unearthed in 1966−67 close to the eastern harbour of the city of Rhodes, the Akandia Bay [1]. There, an artificial channel of 2.15 m depth ran parallel to the ancient city walls, separated by an 11 m wide street running in between [1]. The sidewalls of the conduit were made of at least four layers of poros stone blocks, the same material also employed for the arch [1]. Nearly at its midpoint, the canal is crossed by an 8 m broad arch of wedge-shaped stones which served as a footbridge [1]. The curved opening has a single span of 2.8 m, identical to the width of the canal [1]. Its surprisingly low rise, in the order of 1 to 3−4, gives it the appearance of a segmental or even multi-centered arch, of which it is the only known example in Greek architecture [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103454", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:103455", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s013: period_start ''-500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: period_name None -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:239745", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s006: period_start None -> ''-400''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:341444", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "insert-2026-10-07-006-001: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/8ece5123/Rhodos Epta piges.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
