    ('8ed8a300-acf7-4287-8b86-6a6185e3a8a2'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:8ed8a300-acf7-4287-8b86-6a6185e3a8a2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''7'' restored on Temple Wood, Kilmartin Glen', '6dcd3219252db1178c419d39870546fc', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Stone circle'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 5 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36835", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0044:chunk-0001: description ''Temple Wood is a Neolithic and Bronze Age ritual site in Kilmartin Glen, Argyll, Scotland, that saw approximately 2,000 years of continuous use. Beginning around 3500-3000 BC with a timber circle, an oval setting of 22 standing stones was erected circa 3000 BC, none taller than 1.6 metres. One stone bears a spiral motif paralleled in Irish passage-grave art, indicating prehistoric contact between Kilmartin and Ireland. Around 2000 BC the site transitioned to funerary use, with burial cairns and cist burials containing barbed-and-tanged arrowheads, a decorated Beaker, and red and yellow ochre. Today 13 standing stones remain in a circle about 12 metres in diameter.'' -> \"Temple Wood (or Half Moon Wood) is an ancient site located in Kilmartin Glen, near Kintyre, Argyll, Scotland [1]. The site includes two circles [1]. The southern circle contains a ring of 13 standing stones about 12 metres in diameter [1]. In the centre is a burial cist surrounded by a circle of stones about 3 metres in diameter [1]. According to the Historic Scotland information marker at the site, the southern circle''s first incarnation may have been constructed around 3000 BC [1]. The northern circle is smaller and consists of rounded river stones [1]. In its centre is a single stone; another stone is found on the edge of the circle [1]. This circle may have originated as a timber circle [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:155396", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-019: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Temple_wood_2006.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8ed8a300/Kilmartin Temple Wood Stone Circles 3.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:313955", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-009: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/8ed8a300/Kilmartin Temple Wood Stone Circles 3.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/8ed8a300/Temple_wood_2006.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
