    ('98915fdd-4e67-42aa-8631-53a063cd5109'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:98915fdd-4e67-42aa-8631-53a063cd5109:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Castell Nadolig', 'f98920b1a22c073b2348c0d0feb71260', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81110", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2020:chunk-0001: description ''Castell Nadolig (\"Christmas Castle\") is an Iron Age hillfort in Ceredigion, Wales, about 4 km east of Aberporth. It has two concentric banks, the outer measuring about 230 m in diameter. The fort is famous as the findspot of the Penbryn Spoons -- two ornate cast bronze objects discovered in 1829, dating to c. 50 BC - AD 100, now in the Ashmolean Museum. Only 25 such spoons are known worldwide; they may have been druidic divination tools, with liquid dripped from a perforated spoon onto one divided into quadrants. Geophysical surveys (2019-2021) revealed multiple plough-levelled barrows within and outside the fort.'' -> \"Castell Nadolig (''Christmas Castle'') is an Iron Age hillfort, about 2.5 miles (4 km) east of the village of Aberporth and about 1 mile (2 km) south of the village of Penbryn, in Ceredigion, Wales [1]. The Penbryn Spoons, of the late Iron Age, were found here [1]. The fort, sited on a low summit, is next to the A487 road to the south, which here may be the course of an ancient ridgeway [1]. There are two concentric banks, the outer defence measuring about 230 metres west–east by 185 metres north–south, enclosing 3.8 hectares, and the inner defence measuring about 130 metres by 78 metres, enclosing 0.84 hectares [1]. There is a wide space of about 60 metres between the banks; this is thought to have aided the defence of the site, which is not in a strong position [1]. The surviving ramparts are 2–3 metres high and 7–10 metres wide at the base, now forming parts of hedgebanks [1]. In 1829, two objects of cast bronze were found by a tenant farmer beneath a heap of stones in the hillfort [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104262", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104263", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240671", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240672", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-800''"}]'::jsonb),
