nastery of St Lot is a Byzantine-period monastic site near the Dead Sea in Jordan, at the entrance to a natural cave, which Catholics believed to have been the one where Lot and his daughters sought shelter after Sodom was destroyed [1]. The monastery, dating to the 5th-7th centuries, is centered around a basilical church and overlooks from a steep slope the southeastern Dead Sea and the modern town of Safi in Jordan [1]. The oldest material found inside the cave suggests a sizable Early and Middle Bronze Age presence, followed by Nabataean pottery, which is indicative of a settlement there during the first centuries BC and AD [1]. Two inscriptions on the church''s mosaic floor bear dates from the 7th century: construction in April 606, and a renovation in Xanthikos (roughly May) 691 [1]. Habitation only ceased after the Early Abbasid period [1]. The monastery had a large basilical church 18 x 17 meters long, a reservoir, a hostel complex, and access to a wide range of foods including fruits, meats, and fish from the Red Sea [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104381", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:104382", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s014: period_start ''-3000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157514", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/9e723832/hero.webp'' -> ''https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Lot%27s_cave_1.jpg/1280px-Lot%27s_cave_1.jpg''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240725", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:240726", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s007: period_start None -> ''-3300''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9ed175c7-a706-4a71-b10a-f93a68734afc'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '8', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:9ed175c7-a706-4a71-b10a-f93a68734afc:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: antiquity ''8'' restored on The Gop', '52fe46552d59bb96e3befbff00b21710', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32725", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0001:chunk-0001: description ''A massive Neolithic cairn on Gop Hill in Flintshire, Wales, and the second-largest ancient human-made mound in Britain after Silbury Hill. The oval monument stands 14 m high with a maximum diameter of 101 m, constructed of limestone blocks with drystone walling. Boyd Dawkins excavated in 1886-87 using tunnels and a shaft but found no burial chamber, leaving its function uncertain. The Gop Cave beneath yielded fourteen human skeletons, a stone axe, and Peterborough pottery indicating Neolithic activity. Whether it served as a passage grave, ceremonial platform, or hillfort remains debated.'' -> ''The Gop (Welsh: Coparleni, also known as Gop Cairn or Gop-y-Goleuni) is a Neolithic monument lying within the Clwydian Range, northwest of Trelawnyd, in Flintshire, Wales [1]. Sitting on top of Gop Hill, a natural outcrop of Carboniferous Limestone, the cairn mound is 75-80m in diameter and 12m high [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121606", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121607", "url": "