 of the two stones, weighing an estimated 62 tons, and along with three others formed a four-sided cove [1]. Excavations carried out jointly by the Universities of Leicester, Newport and Southampton in 2000 revealed the socket holes for the other stones which were tightly placed close to Adam [1]. The cove had been open on its south-eastern side which faced towards the nearby South Street barrow, 130m away [1]. The other stones were removed in the post-medieval period by a local landowner [1]. Adam fell over in 1911 and was re-erected by Maud Cunnington in 1912, at the direction of the Wiltshire Archaeological Society [1]. She also found a Beaker inhumation of a middle-aged man buried close by the stone [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105057", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s015: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2900''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Longstones%2C_Geograph.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:314888", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-010: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a06ad29e/Longstones, Geograph.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:384333", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4402:chunk-0001: description \"The Longstones are two standing stones, one of which is the remains of a prehistoric ''cove'' of standing stones, at grid reference SU089693, close to Beckhampton in Avebury parish [1]. Two stones are visible, known as ''Adam'' and ''Eve'', although the latter is more likely to have been a stone that formed part of the 