t consisting of a massive dry-stone wall, with a well or cistern in the middle [1]. The light-coloured stone wall gives the White Caterthun its name [1]. The Brown Caterthun, on the east, consists of a series of earthen embankments [1]. There is little evidence of settlement, agriculture or water supply here, so the purpose of the earthworks is uncertain [1]. Both Caterthuns show several entrances to the summit that radiate outwards, like the spokes on a wheel [1]. From radio-carbon dating, the Brown Caterthun appears to have been built and modified over several centuries in the latter half of the first millennium BC [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157576", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a1118dbb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a1118dbb/White_Caterthun.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
