ished by the Urartian king Rusa I (r. 735–714 BCE) in honor of the weather god Theispas to \"demonstrate the power of Biaina [the Urartian capital, modern Van] and to cow her enemies.\" Some sources refer to the site as \"Teisheba\" or other variations of the Urartian god\\''s name [1]. In the post-Urartian period Odzaberd was a regional center, as evidenced by recovered pottery styles and the use of mudbricks [1]. The Odzaberd site is on the southern shore of Lake Sevan near the town of Tsovinar [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121646", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: lat ''40.168024646758'' -> ''40.1528''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:121647", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s007: lon ''45.45972198440738'' -> ''45.4953''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157638", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Overlooking_Teyseba.JPG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325320", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a29d5e41/Overlooking Teyseba.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
