    ('a52e274f-3e08-44b5-a03f-1d564fede9a2'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:a52e274f-3e08-44b5-a03f-1d564fede9a2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Dipylon', '53542aff2633904a57156c05ee904258', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Gate/archway/bridge'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 26 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29043", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0230:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-478''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29044", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0230:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Gate/archway/bridge''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35929", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-478'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81150", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2022:chunk-0001: description ''The Dipylon was the principal gateway in the ancient walls of Classical Athens, situated in the Kerameikos district[1]. This monumental gate served as the starting point for the grand procession of the Great Panathenaea[1][2]. Erected in 478 BC as part of Themistocles\\'' fortification of Athens and rebuilt in the 300s BC, it was described as \"the largest gate of the ancient world\"[1]. The gateway remained in use until the 3rd century AD, controlling access to the ancient cemetery and roads connecting Athens with the rest of Greece[1][2].'' -> ''The Dipylon (Greek: Δίπυλον, \"Two-Gated\") was the main gate in the city wall of Classical Athens [1]. Located in the modern suburb of Kerameikos, it led to the namesake ancient cemetery [1]. The gate was of major ceremonial significance as the starting point of the procession of the Great Panathenaea, and accordingly it was a large, monumental structure [1]. Erected in 478 BC as part of Themistocles\\'' fortification of Athens and rebuilt in the 300s BC, it remained standing and in use until the 3rd century AD [1]. The Dipylon was the \"largest gate of the ancient world\", with a surface of c. 1,800 square metres [1]. The original, Themistoclean-era gate largely established the final shape of the structure and covered the same area as the Hellenistic structure, and was built of clay bricks on top of a pedestal of Poros limestone blocks [1]. The Hellenistic gate complex was an altogether more massive and elaborate affair, being built of large finely hewn ashlar blocks [1]. The northern cityward gate is still visible, but the southern one has been destroyed through the construction of a modern canal [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157732", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a52e274f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a52e274f/Dipylon, Kerameikos, Athens, 225754.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('a52e274f-3e08-44b5-a03f-1d564fede9a2'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '29', '32', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:a52e274f-3e08-44b5-a03f-1d564fede9a2:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: rarity_score ''32'' restored on Dipylon', '53542aff2633904a57156c05ee904258', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 29 from mystery 5, cultural_influence 7"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29043", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0230:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-478''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29044", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0230:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Gate/archway/bridge''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35929", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-478'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81150", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2022:chunk-0001: description ''The Dipylon was the principal gateway in the ancient walls of Classical Athens, situated in the Kerameikos district[1]. This monumental gate served as the starting point for the grand procession of the Great Panathenaea[1][2]. Erected in 478 BC as part of Themistocles\\'' fortification of Athens and rebuilt in the 300s BC, it was described as \"the largest gate of the ancient world\"[1]. The gateway remained in use until the 3rd century AD, controlling access to the ancient cemetery and roads connecting Athens with the rest of Greece[1][2].'' -> ''The Dipylon (Greek: Δίπυλον, \"Two-Gated\") was the main gate in the city wall of Classical Athens [1]. Located in the modern suburb of Kerameikos, it led to the namesake ancient cemetery [1]. The gate was of major ceremonial significance as the starting point of the procession of the Great Panathenaea, and accordingly it was a large, monumental structure [1]. Erected in 478 BC as part of Themistocles\\'' fortification of Athens and rebuilt in the 300s BC, it remained standing and in use until the 3rd century AD [1]. The Dipylon was the \"largest gate of the ancient world\", with a surface of c. 1,800 square metres [1]. The original, Themistoclean-era gate largely established the final shape of the structure and covered the same area as the Hellenistic structure, and was built of clay bricks on top of a pedestal of Poros limestone blocks [1]. The Hellenistic gate complex was an altogether more massive and elaborate affair, being built of large finely hewn ashlar blocks [1]. The northern cityward gate is still visible, but the southern one has been destroyed through the construction of a modern canal [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:157732", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-022: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/a52e274f/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/a52e274f/Dipylon, Kerameikos, Athens, 225754.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
