rthern Ireland [1]. The circle measures around 35 m in diameter and includes 50 or more small, upright stones, with a maximum height of 1.8 m [1]. The excavation was conducted between 20 - 27 September 1937 and 7 - 16 June 1938 by Professor Albert Giffen, a Dutch archaeologist, and Miss Mary McMurray Gaffikin, an archaeology expert from Newcastle, County Down [1]. The excavation uncovered a partially curbed long cairn 21 metres in length by 9 metres wide within the stone circle [1]. There were cists at both the east and west ends with the west end later augmented with two more chambers [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105926", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105927", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:158480", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-023: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/4 Ballynoe Stone Circle 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315687", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/4 Ballynoe Stone Circle 1.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/ac2c74ff/Ballynoe_(27),_September_2009.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
