6 metres long by 2.7 metres wide, and consists of vertical stone slabs, divided by a now-broken cross slab [1]. The cairn originally had a stone circle surrounding it, with four portal stones; two of these portal stones still remain [1]. The site is protected as a scheduled ancient monument [1]. While the southern side of the main chamber was originally a single, 18-foot-long stone, it was split in 1843 by a picknicker''s bonfire [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159102", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Bridestones_1.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/aea029cf/Bridestones.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315741", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/aea029cf/Bridestones.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/aea029cf/Bridestones_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
