 site, with a 35 ft gap between the edge of the mere and the defences; however when the hill fort was built the water-level would have been higher and closer to the scarp, offering the site natural defences [1]. Ramparts were thrown up around the south-west and north sides of the site, creating an arc, and a ditch was created in front of the rampart [1]. In 1960, Oakmere hill fort was excavated by archaeologist James Forde-Johnston; the excavation focused on the site''s southern defences and showed that the ditch was 50 ft wide at its zenith and originally 10 ft deep in places [1]. The structure was made a Scheduled Ancient Monument in 1995 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:105999", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106000", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251975", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386663", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386664", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
