    ('b07c4d65-158e-4e9d-9817-f39161b7516d'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:b07c4d65-158e-4e9d-9817-f39161b7516d:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''4'' restored on Duddo Five Stones', 'd501ede4fd1f4ba888a32ebb413b48e7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 37 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86790", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2152:chunk-0001: description ''Duddo Five Stones is a Neolithic stone circle in North Northumberland, England, situated approximately 6 km (4 mi) south of the Scottish Border[1]. The site originally featured seven stones; excavations in the 1890s revealed empty socket holes for two missing stones on the western side[1]. Known as the Four Stones until 1903, a fifth stone was re-erected that year to improve the skyline[1]. The surviving stones are constructed from soft sandstone that has become deeply fissured through natural weathering[2]. The circle dates to approximately 3300-2200 BC[2].'' -> ''The Duddo Five Stones is a stone circle north of Duddo in North Northumberland, approximately 4 miles South of the Scottish Border [1]. The stones were known as the Four Stones until 1903, when the fifth stone was re-erected to improve the skyline [1]. There were originally seven stones, the empty sockets of two stones being found on the western side during excavation in the 1890s [1]. The stones are formed of a local soft pink Doddington sandstone [1]. The site of the Duddo Stones offers panoramic views of the Cheviot Hills to the South and the Lammermuir Hills to the north [1]. The location was the subject of an archeological investigation in 2008 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106033", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106034", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159153", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b07c4d65/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b07c4d65/DuddoJune18g.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
