 including scrapers, awls, and blades — as well as a quantity of pottery fragments, confirming a date in the Copper Age of the fourth millennium BC [1]. Further documented finds at the site include obsidian stones, sandstone vessels, burnt ornaments, necklaces, and beads of metal, shell, and bone, alongside fan scrapers [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159155", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/Rajajil standing columns, al-Jouf, 6th - 5th millenia BCE (1).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315767", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/Rajajil standing columns, al-Jouf, 6th - 5th millenia BCE (1).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/أعمدة_الرجاجيل_2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b0a89bee-ba85-4f69-a396-2d30707ff1e7'::uuid, 'total_power', '31', '30', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:b0a89bee-ba85-4f69-a396-2d30707ff1e7:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: total_power ''30'' restored on Columns of Rajajil', '4f7c7d158ce208393d765b0ab5a7e884', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 31 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 8 + 8 + 7 + 7 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37271", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0051:chunk-0001: description \"The Columns of Rajajil are an archaeological site in Saudi Arabia''s Al Jawf Region, comprising 50 groups of upright sandstone pillars near Qara south of Sakakah. Some pillars exceed 3 meters in height and are about 60 cm thick. The site is thought to be approximately 6,000 years old. Some columns bear Thamudic inscriptions.[1]\" -> ''The Columns of Rajajil is an archaeological site of sandstone pillars in the Al Jawf Region of Saudi Arabia, dated to the Chalcolithic period approximately 6,000 years ago [1]. Sometimes popularly described as \"the Stonehenge of Saudi Arabia,\" the site comprises 54 groups of trimmed stone pillars, each group containing between two and 19 pillars, distributed across a wide sandy plain in the Qara suburb southeast of Sakakah [1]. The site is named Rajajil from a local Arabic term meaning \"men,\" as the standing columns are said to resemble a gathering of human figures when viewed from a distance [1]. Excavations carried out by the Saudi General Administration of Antiquities and Museums between 1975 and 1976 recovered stone tools — including scrapers, awls, and blades — as well as a quantity of pottery fragments, confirming a date in the Copper Age of the fourth millennium BC [1]. Further documented finds at the site include obsidian stones, sandstone vessels, burnt ornaments, necklaces, and beads of metal, shell, and bone, alongside fan scrapers [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159155", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/Rajajil standing columns, al-Jouf, 6th - 5th millenia BCE (1).webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:315767", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-011: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/Rajajil standing columns, al-Jouf, 6th - 5th millenia BCE (1).webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b0a89bee/أعمدة_الرجاجيل_2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('b10227e6-8a83-4290-8503-ec037e4bc3c3'::uuid, 'antiquity', '5', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:b10227e6-8a83-4290-8503-ec037e4bc3c3:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: antiquity ''7'' restored on Devil''s Jumps, Treyford', 'a5a123e9583f61debb5d5dab0018c6c8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 5 from period_start None"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37295", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0052:chunk-0001: description \"The Devil''s Jumps are a group of five large Bronze Age bell barrows on the South Downs, 1.2 km southeast of Treyford in West Sussex, considered the best-preserved barrow cemetery on the South Downs. The barrows range from 20-26 metres in central diameter with overall mound diameters of 37-45 metres and heights of 2.7-5.3 metres. Their linear alignment runs northeast to southwest, oriented with sunset on Midsummer Day. Excavations in 1853 recovered cremated human bone from ground surfaces between the mounds. Listed as a Scheduled Ancient Monument and Local Nature Reserve, the site provides valuable insight into Bronze Age burial traditions and social organization in southern England.\" -> \"The Devil''s Jumps are a group of five large bell barrows situated on the South Downs 1.2 kilometres south-east of Treyford in the county of West Sussex in southern England [1]. Most barrows along the South Downs have been damaged by agriculture and treasure hunters but the Devil''s Jumps are considered to be the best preserved Bronze Age barrow group in Sussex [1]. The barrows are laid out in a line running approximately south-east to north-west [1]. The five barrows vary in diameter from 26 to 34 metres and stand up to 4.8 metres high [1]. The Devil''s Jumps have been dated to the Bronze Age and they are believed to be between three and four thousand years old [1]. The Devil''s Jumps were explored in the 19th century, when bones were found in two of the mounds, although some of the barrows contained no cremated remains at all [1]. The main line of five barrows is aligned with sunset on Midsummer Day [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106042", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:106043", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s016: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/Devils Jumps Treyford 22.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251991", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251992", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:324495", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-004-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/Devils Jumps Treyford 22.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:325988", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b10227e6/Devils Jumps Treyford 1.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351842", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC