.\" -> \"Wet Withens is a Bronze Age henge on Eyam Moor in the Derbyshire Peak District [1]. On gritstone moorland over 300 metres above sea level, the site has clear views of the valley of the River Derwent and Higger Tor [1]. The earthen bank of Wet Withens is over 30m in diameter, making it the largest embanked stone circle in Derbyshire [1]. The ''Chair Stone'' is the tallest and most prominent stone with a ledge cut out of its face, appearing as a seat [1]. About 10m north of the henge is a large stone cairn (known as Eyam Moor Barrow) about 27m long and 1m high [1]. Near the middle of the cairn is an unusual cup-marked stone [1]. The cairn''s shape indicates that it was likely to have been a prehistoric burial barrow [1]. Wet Withens is also known as Eyam Moor 1 because there are 2 smaller stone circles about 650m to the east [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159199", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-024: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Wet_Withens_-_geograph.org.uk_-_59759.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b1410fda/Wet Withens - geograph.org.uk - 59759.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251995", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:251996", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s015: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351846", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:351847", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s007: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:386671", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s003: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change