e friends, who entered through a narrow sinkhole known as \"La Mina\" [1]. The cave is divided into two main parts known as Nerja I and Nerja II [1]. Skeletal remains found in the caverns indicate that they were inhabited from about 25,000 BC up until the Bronze Age [1]. Cave paintings from the Paleolithic and post-Paleolithic eras have been discovered on the walls of the cave [1]. For about 4,000 years from 25,000 BC the caves were used seasonally by a small group of humans, and were occupied by cave hyena during the periods that the humans were absent [1]. By 21,000 BC the human population had taken up year-round residence in the caves and had increased in number [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159982", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b5e251e7/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b5e251e7/Cueva_de_Nerja_2020.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:332862", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b5e251e7/Cueva_de_Nerja_2020.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b5e251e7/Cuevas de Nerja.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
