    ('b69f338c-fb2b-4860-9935-61e93e27ddf3'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:b69f338c-fb2b-4860-9935-61e93e27ddf3:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Penrhos Feilw Standing Stones', 'b39898a9bad7c82d0b3be94fb8ae5c35', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:86966", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2157:chunk-0001: description ''The Penrhos Feilw Standing Stones are a pair of Bronze Age monoliths located 1.75 miles southwest of Holyhead on Holy Island, west of Anglesey in north-west Wales. Each stone stands approximately 3 m (10 ft) tall, about 0.9 m wide at the base, and only 20-30 cm thick, set roughly 3.3 m apart on open grassland. Erected around 2000-1500 BC, their purpose remains unknown -- they may have held ritual significance, served as a tribal gathering marker, or represent an unfinished monument. Local tradition suggests a cist burial once existed between the stones, though no supporting evidence has been found.'' -> ''The Penrhos Feilw Standing Stones are a pair of standing stones on Holy Island west of Anglesey in north-west Wales [1]. They are thought to date from the Bronze Age but their origins and purpose are unclear [1]. The stones are located behind the farmhouse of Plas Meilw, some 2 km southwest of Holyhead and a similar distance south of Holyhead Mountain [1]. The exposed part of each stone is about 3 m high and 1 m broad at the base, but only about 20 cm thick [1]. They are situated about 3 m apart on an open grassy place above Porth Dafarch between two low hills [1]. They are aligned along their long axes in a northeasterly/southwesterly direction [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:159996", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Penrhos_Feilw_-_geograph.org.uk_-_216.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b69f338c/Penrhos Feilw Standing Stones facing away from the road.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
