    ('b7cc5073-ffca-4cd3-a168-3062908d4959'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:b7cc5073-ffca-4cd3-a168-3062908d4959:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Beacon Hill, West Sussex', '1038acd664154ab15bafdc3727fe8971', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87000", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2158:chunk-0001: description ''Beacon Hill, also known as Harting Beacon, is a hillfort on the South Downs in West Sussex, England [1]. Located in the parish of Elsted and Treyford within Chichester District, the hilltop enclosure dates to the Late Bronze Age, from the 8th to the 6th centuries BC [1]. The hillfort defences were renewed during the Late Iron Age [1]. The site is a Scheduled Ancient Monument [1].'' -> ''Beacon Hill, also known as Harting Beacon, is a hillfort on the South Downs, in the county of West Sussex in southern England [1]. The hilltop enclosure is dated to the Late Bronze Age, from the 8th to 6th centuries BC [1]. The hillfort defences were renewed during the Late Iron Age [1]. The fort extends approximately 400 metres east-west by approximately 500 metres north-south, and covers an area of approximately 10 hectares [1]. Archaeological investigations of the hillfort took place from 1948 to 1952, and from 1976 to 1977 [1]. As well as the Bronze Age remains, the area of the fort also includes an Anglo-Saxon burial mound, and the foundations of a late 18th-century telegraph station [1]. The hillfort is situated on the top of the hill and is defined by a single rampart and a flat-bottomed ditch, which survives as a low bank up to 6 metres wide [1]. The rampart was originally faced with timber, with timber palisades acting as retaining walls on both the inside and the outside [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:160021", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-025: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/b7cc5073/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/b7cc5073/Beacon_Hill_fort_1.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
