    ('bdbe5ee5-28a4-4f75-9777-e9d733738451'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:bdbe5ee5-28a4-4f75-9777-e9d733738451:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Uffington Castle', 'c203e7ec1835e1bf59814b330941a851', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87144", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2162:chunk-0001: description ''Uffington Castle is an Early Iron Age univallate hillfort in Oxfordshire, dating to approximately 800-500 BC. It covers about 3.2 hectares and is surrounded by two earth banks separated by a ditch[1]. The original defensive ditch was V-shaped with a small box rampart in front and a larger one behind; timber posts stood on the ramparts[1]. Later, the ditch was deepened and the extra material dumped on top of the ramparts to increase their size[1]. A parapet wall of sarsen stones lined the top of the innermost rampart[1]. The site has two entrances—the western one serving as the main access, while the eastern entrance was blocked a few centuries after construction[1]. Located on the Berkshire Downs, it lies close to the Uffington White Horse geoglyph on White Horse Hill[1].'' -> ''Uffington Castle is an early Iron Age (with underlying Bronze Age) univallate hillfort in Oxfordshire, England [1]. It covers about 3.2 ha and is surrounded by two earth banks separated by a ditch with an entrance in the western end [1]. The original defensive ditch was V-shaped with a small box rampart in front and a larger one behind it [1]. Timber posts stood on the ramparts [1]. Later the ditch was deepened and the extra material dumped on top of the ramparts to increase their size [1]. A parapet wall of sarsen stones lined the top of the innermost rampart [1]. Isolated postholes were found inside the fort but no evidence of buildings [1]. The hillfort is a Scheduled Monument, and was included in the Ancient Monuments Protection Act 1882 as one of the first 68 sites in Britain and Ireland to receive legal protection [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:160701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-026: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Trig_point_on_Whitehorse_Hill_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1370581.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/bdbe5ee5/Uffington castle II (1303903611).webp''"}]'::jsonb),
