    ('bf395f91-b77b-460f-802b-20cf52a73bfb'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:bf395f91-b77b-460f-802b-20cf52a73bfb:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''7'' restored on Waun Mawn', '57f1aff484f638aa8d6d0f5451141198', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Stone circle'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 5 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29267", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0262:chunk-0001: period_start ''-5000'' -> ''-3400''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30495", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''< 4500 BC'' -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37435", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0055:chunk-0001: description \"Waun Mawn (Welsh for ''peat moor'') is a Neolithic stone circle site in the Preseli Mountains of Pembrokeshire, Wales. Following excavations in 2018, it was identified as a possible dismantled stone circle measuring approximately 110 metres (360 ft) in diameter, making it the fifth largest in Britain after Avebury, Stanton Drew, Karl Lofts, and Long Meg, and slightly larger than the Ring of Brodgar[1]. Located around 1.6 km southwest of Brynberian on the southern slopes of Cnwc yr Hŷdd (339m).\" -> ''Waun Mawn is a megalithic site in the Preseli Mountains of Pembrokeshire, Wales [1]. Following excavations in 2018, it became the site of a supposed dismantled Neolithic stone circle [1]. The site is located around 1 mile to the southwest of Brynberian [1]. During 2017 and 2018, excavations by the UCL team of archaeologist Mike Parker Pearson led to a proposal that the site had originally housed a 110 m diameter stone circle of the same size as the ditch at Stonehenge [1]. Following soil dating of the sediments within the postulated stone holes, via optically stimulated luminescence, it has been argued, by Parker Pearson, that the circle of stones was built c. 3400–3200 BC and then, before 2120 BC, was disassembled, dragged across land and reassembled at Stonehenge in Wiltshire, some 140 mi distant [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:160772", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-026: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Standing_stone_and_lying_stone_near_Cnwc_yr_Hydd_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4213876.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/bf395f91/hero.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
