and have diameters of 35 metres, 42 metres and 33 metres [1]. The middle circle, the largest is slightly elliptical [1]. In the middle circle 14 stones survive out of 28 [1]. The stones show clear traces of being hammered smooth [1]. Ralegh Radford excavated the site in the 1930s, and partly restored the two northern circles by re-erecting some stones and placing marker stones in the positions of those missing [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:107936", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:107937", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161383", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Hurlers2.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers 01.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242743", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:242744", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s009: period_start None -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:316695", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers 01.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c2984c15/Hurlers2.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
