    ('c34bf073-ed1e-4626-8cc8-8cbd724d9b93'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:c34bf073-ed1e-4626-8cc8-8cbd724d9b93:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on St Breock Downs Monolith', '2f24f43a2ee20c16f1d58e989e9d8892', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87288", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2167:chunk-0001: description ''St Breock Downs Monolith is a prehistoric standing stone (menhir) on the summit of St Breock Downs in Cornwall, England [1]. It is the largest and heaviest megalithic stone in Cornwall, weighing approximately 16.5 tonnes and measuring 4.92 metres in length [1][2]. The stone is made from local Devonian shale with extensive feldspar veining and stands about 3 metres tall above ground level [1][2].'' -> ''St Breock Downs Monolith is the largest and heaviest prehistoric Menhir in Cornwall [1]. It stands on the summit of St Breock Downs [1]. The stone is made from the local Devonian shale which has extensive feldspar veining, and it is estimated to weigh around 16.5 tonnes [1]. It is 4.92 metres long and stands to a height of just over 3 metres above ground level [1]. It stands on a low stone mound or cairn with a diameter of around 10 metres [1]. It is believed to be Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age (around 2500-1500 BC) [1]. It fell over in 1945, and was re-erected in 1956 after a small excavation had been carried out [1]. The excavation showed that the stone stood in a setting of quartz pebbles below which were two small hollows [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161416", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/SaintBreockDownsLongstone_ancientAndModern.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c34bf073/St Breock Downs Monolith.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:316712", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c34bf073/St Breock Downs Monolith.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c34bf073/SaintBreockDownsLongstone_ancientAndModern.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
