    ('c5a5ae3a-46fd-45e6-8d02-45abfdee24aa'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:c5a5ae3a-46fd-45e6-8d02-45abfdee24aa:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''6'' restored on Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni', 'b742d124013fb9de45e847623e107bdb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Cemetery'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45211", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0106:chunk-0001: description ''The Mycenaean Cemetery of Voudeni (1500-1050 BC) is part of the Patras Mycenaean Park in Western Greece, located 7 km northeast of Patras near the settlement of Voudeni [1]. The site covers 72.84 hectares at an altitude of 220 meters and contains 78 carved tombs alongside ruins of a prehistoric settlement possibly identified with ancient Mesatis [1]. First excavated in 1923 by Nikolaos Kyparissis, the cemetery yielded numerous artifacts including vases, clay statuettes, bronze tools, weapons, and jewelry, now displayed at the Archaeological Museum of Patras [1]. The site has been transformed into a recreational park [1].'' -> ''The Mycenaean cemetery of Voudeni along with the Mycenaean settlement at the locality of Bortzi, comprise the Patras Mycenaean Park, located near the settlement of Voudeni of the Municipality of Patras in Western Greece [1]. The archaeological site of a total area of 180 acres is located at an altitude of 220 meters, 7 kilometers northeast of Patras [1]. It includes 78 carved tombs and the ruins of a prehistoric settlement, possibly identified with that of ancient Mesatis [1]. Most of the explored graves were carved in circular, square, horseshoe or quadrilateral form with a dome, of varying dimensions often housing multiple burials [1]. All of the tombs revealed objects of everyday life, such as vases, jewellery, tools, weapons and utensils [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108005", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:108006", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s018: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:161481", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-027: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/%CE%92%CE%9F%CE%A5%CE%9D%CE%A4%CE%95%CE%9D%CE%97_09.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c5a5ae3a/Tomb75_interior.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:316762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-012: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c5a5ae3a/Tomb75_interior.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c5a5ae3a/ΒΟΥΝΤΕΝΗ_09.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
