    ('c72fb36a-0726-4975-9d7a-edc5bd59a66f'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:c72fb36a-0726-4975-9d7a-edc5bd59a66f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Acaray', 'cdd33a68c2329c87384ec6e559c48c57', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28534", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0155:chunk-0002: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-900''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36004", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-900'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37505", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0057:chunk-0001: description \"Acaray is an ancient fortress in the Huaura River Valley on Peru''s near north coast [1]. The impressive structure sits atop three hilltops, each surrounded by perimeter defensive walls featuring parapets and bastions [2]. Radio carbon dating indicates the fortress was originally constructed during the Early Horizon period around 900-200 BC, with later occupation during the Late Intermediate Period, before being abandoned between 1000-1470 AD [3]. Excavations revealed that activities at Acaray extended beyond defensive purposes, yielding evidence of both conflict and ritual activities [4][5].\" -> ''Acaray, also known as the Fortress of Acaray, is an archaeological site located in the Huaura River Valley on the near north coast of Peru [1]. Attention was called to the site early on by the German archaeologist Hans Horkheimer, who wrote about Acaray in 1962 in the Peruvian magazine, Caretas, published in Lima [1]. Calling it the Fortaleza de Huaura, he deemed Acaray to be a true fortress, unlike the more well-known neighboring site of Paramonga in the Pativilca River Valley, whose defensive nature has been questioned [1]. During the 1970s interest in Acaray increased, and the first work by archaeologists was initiated at the fortress [1]. In 2004, North American archaeologist Margaret Brown Vega, of the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, began research at Acaray, carrying out intensive mapping, surface analysis, and excavations, which lasted for two years [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:326040", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c72fb36a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c72fb36a/Vista a Sector B 8-10-04.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
