long the highway to Ayacucho known as the Via de los Libertadores [1]. The site was most likely built at the end of the 15th century during the reign of the Inca king Pachacuti Inca Yupanqui also known as Pachacutec [1]. The site owes its name to the abundant use of colors on the walls [1]. Thanks to favorable environmental conditions, many walls at Tambo, both internal and external, retain enough residual colored paint to accurately reconstruct what the original wall painting would have been like [1]. The site consists of several structures around a large central plaza [1]. The central plaza is shaped like a trapezoid with its largest side being 150 m long [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:162100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-028: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/TamboColorado-colours.PNG'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/Tambo Colorado, Peru.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:334777", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-009-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/Tambo Colorado, Peru.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/c947e9b6/TamboColorado-colours.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
