    ('cea5eebe-1a8a-4132-8867-12102672ecc0'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:cea5eebe-1a8a-4132-8867-12102672ecc0:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Maiden Castle, Cheshire', '6884d0967fbe7eb7b3db1910399dc15b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29190", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0251:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-600''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36025", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-600'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81326", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2028:chunk-0001: description ''Maiden Castle is an Iron Age hill fort in Cheshire, one of only seven fortified settlements in the county[1]. Constructed around 600 BC, it was occupied until the Roman conquest of Britain in the 1st century AD[1]. The Cornovii tribe are recorded as occupying the surrounding area during this period, though their presence lacks archaeological verification due to an absence of distinctive pottery or metalwork[1]. The site has since been quarried and used for military exercises[1]. It is protected as a Scheduled Ancient Monument, owned by the National Trust, and open to visitors despite being classified as at high risk from erosion[1].'' -> ''Maiden Castle is an Iron Age hill fort, one of many fortified hill-top settlements constructed across Britain during the Iron Age, but one of only seven in the county of Cheshire in northern England [1]. The hill fort was probably occupied from its construction in 600 BC until the Roman conquest of Britain in the 1st century AD [1]. Maiden Castle sits on a slight promontory which is the highest point of a plateau on Bickerton Hill [1]. Maiden Castle has no artificial defences on its northern and western sides because of the naturally steep slope of the hill, but there are two artificial banks, 35 ft (11 m) apart [1]. Radiocarbon dating indicates that the ramparts defending Maiden Castle were built in around 600 BC [1]. Built from earth and timber, the inner rampart was originally 20 ft (6.1 m) wide, with a revetment of dry stone walling behind the bank, and at least 10 ft (3.0 m) high [1]. The outer bank was originally 25 ft (7.6 m) wide and about 10 ft (3.0 m) high [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:317561", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-013: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/cea5eebe/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/cea5eebe/Maiden_Castle_summit_Bickerton.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
