that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age [1]. Positioned in a dry valley, the circle was erected on the southern end of a field that contained a range of natural sarsens [1]. Extensive flint knapping took place at the site during the Late Neolithic or Early Bronze Age [1]. The earliest known report of the site came from a Mr Falkner, who discovered it in 1840 while riding in the area [1]. The site was excavated in 2002 in an archaeological project led by Mark Gillings and Joshua Pollard [1]. Falkner''s Circle is 750m south-east of Avebury [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109748", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:109749", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s020: period_start ''-2300'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254099", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_name None -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:254100", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s020: period_start None -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353706", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:353707", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-17a_fields-wd5-s009: period_start ''-4000'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
