    ('dfab4856-d41f-4228-a94a-e32822c59a0f'::uuid, 'mystery', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:dfab4856-d41f-4228-a94a-e32822c59a0f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''1'' restored on Hunsbury Hill', '0da8d875052274f7a9507ec29e147658', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 4 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 86 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2187:chunk-0001: description ''An Iron Age hillfort 3 km southwest of Northampton, also known as Danes Camp, with defences built between the 7th and 4th centuries BC. The 1.6-hectare site features a deep ditch originally 8 metres deep and vitrified ramparts -- a rare phenomenon in England where the entire inner rampart was purposely burnt at intense heat. Excavations uncovered over 300 pits, 159 quern stones, the decorated Hunsbury Scabbard, bronze brooches, and evidence of a chariot burial, forming one of the largest Iron Age artifact collections in the Midlands. It is a scheduled monument.'' -> ''Hunsbury Hill is an Iron Age hill fort two miles south-west of the centre of the town of Northampton in the county of Northamptonshire [1]. It is also known as Danes Camp [1]. It is probable that defences were built at Hunsbury Hill between the 7th and 4th centuries BC [1]. Ironstone extraction began at the hill fort in about 1883 [1]. Many of the fort\\''s internal features were destroyed, but the work revealed up to 300 pits which, according to the curator of Northampton Museum in 1887, contained \"numerous artefacts that now comprise one of the finest collections ... of Prehistoric antiquities in England\" [1]. The finds included iron weapons and tools, bronze brooches, pottery, glass and around 159 quern-stones [1]. All were given to the town\\''s museum [1]. Hunsbury Hill fort is a designated Scheduled Monument [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110490", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:110491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s021: period_start ''-1500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:164383", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-031: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/dfab4856/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/dfab4856/Hunsbury_Hill_Digital_Terrain_Model.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243818", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:243819", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s010: period_start None -> ''-700''"}]'::jsonb),
