n Martin Down, near the village of Martin, in Hampshire [1]. It is near the boundaries with Dorset and Wiltshire [1]. The site is a scheduled monument, and it is one of several archaeological features on Martin Down, such as Bokerley Dyke [1]. The enclosure is the original example of a type of prehistoric feature, the \"Martin Down style enclosure\": they are small enclosures of the Bronze Age, area often less than 0.3 hectares, considered to be domestic settlements [1]. The rectangular enclosure has internal dimensions of about 90 metres south-west to north-east by 63 metres [1]. It has a single bank of width up to 10 metres and up to 0.7 metres above the interior, and there is an external ditch [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112238", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112239", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112240", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Earthwork''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302214", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_name None -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:302215", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04a_fields-wd4-s001: period_start None -> ''-2000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387759", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387760", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_start ''-2000'' -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f784a924-a2de-42ae-b530-4b292753fa8f'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:f784a924-a2de-42ae-b530-4b292753fa8f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Overton Hill', 'e06a83e309cf82aaa47b5c3e7694c3e4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Barrow'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 19 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88492", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2202:chunk-0001: description ''Overton Hill is a 174-metre hill at the southern edge of the Marlborough Downs in Wiltshire, England. It is notable as the start point of the Ridgeway National Trail, which follows an ancient trackway. The hill contains several scheduled archaeological sites including Bronze Age round barrows and the site of The Sanctuary, a Neolithic wooden and stone circle connected to Avebury by the West Kennet Avenue. The area forms part of the Stonehenge and Avebury UNESCO World Heritage Site.'' -> ''Overton Hill is a 571 ft hill at the southern edge of the Marlborough Downs in Wiltshire [1]. It lies just west of the village of West Overton and about 4 miles west from the town of Marlborough [1]. The hill is notable for being the start point of the Ridgeway National Trail, which follows an ancient trackway [1]. The hill is part of the Avebury section of the Stonehenge and Avebury World Heritage Site [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:126648", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s011: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 B