of West Sussex [1]. Consisting of a roughly circular low earthen rampart surrounded by a ditch, Chanctonbury Ring is thought to date to the late Bronze Age or early Iron Age [1]. The hill fort is roughly circular, enclosing about 1.5 hectares (3.7 acres) [1]. Chanctonbury Ring was investigated by archeologists in excavations carried out in 1869, 1909, 1977 and 1988–91 [1]. Evidence for human activity in the Neolithic and early Bronze Age has been found, but not settlement or occupation [1]. Around five hundred years after its initial abandonment, Chanctonbury Ring was reused as a religious site from the mid-first century AD [1]. At least two Romano-Celtic temples were built in the interior, probably some time during the second century, while the bank and ditch were reused to form a temenos or \"sacred precinct\" [1]. Mosaic fragments from Chanctonbury Ring can be found at the Horsham Museum [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:166762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-034: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_bank_south-west_view.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320009", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_bank_south-west_view.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/f9bb5abb/Chanctonbury_Ring_south-east_view.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
