    ('fc553d2e-73db-4bfb-b3a7-ba8f95597304'::uuid, 'empire_count', '1', '0', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:fc553d2e-73db-4bfb-b3a7-ba8f95597304:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: empire_count ''0'' restored on Bury Walls', 'b959759ed48c2565e9d98aca7910cf12', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 1 from tag_site(52.84291385744504, -2.628696933385039, period_start None)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88618", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2206:chunk-0001: description \"Bury Walls is a large multivallate Iron Age hillfort near Weston-under-Redcastle in Shropshire, designated as a scheduled monument. Positioned on a south-facing sandstone promontory at 160 metres elevation, it encloses approximately 8 hectares with dimensions of 520 by 380 metres, making it one of the largest hillforts in the region. Large multivallate hillforts of this class are rare nationally, with only about 50 examples recorded across Britain. Archaeological work in 1930 uncovered rectangular stone structures in the interior originally thought to be medieval, but now considered almost certainly Roman in date. The defensive circuit encompasses a natural spring, and the fort''s size suggests it served as a major community centre for economic and social activities.\" -> ''Bury Walls is an Iron Age hillfort about 1 mile south-east of Weston-under-Redcastle, in Shropshire [1]. The fort is on a promontory facing south-west, part of the southern escarpment of a sandstone ridge [1]. The enclosure is about 520 metres north to south and 380 metres east to west; the area within is about 8 hectares [1]. There are steep slopes on all sides except the north, where there are two massive ramparts and ditches, the inner rampart being about 7.8 metres above the interior [1]. Elsewhere there is a single rampart around the edge of the promontory, about 3.0 metres along the east side and up to 1.8 metres along the west side [1]. In 1930 there was some excavation of the site by E. W. Bowcock [1]. Near the centre of the interior, foundations of a building were discovered, thought at the time to be medieval, but more recently thought to be the remains of a Romano-Celtic temple [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112336", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112337", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255093", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255094", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387769", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387770", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
