orded by the antiquarian William Stukeley although the stones were destroyed by local farmers in the 1720s [1]. The Sanctuary underwent archaeological excavation by Maud and Ben Cunnington in 1930 [1]. The first stage of activity at the site, some time around 3000 BCE, consisted of a ring of eight wooden posts 4.5 metres across, with a central post, presumed to be a round hut [1]. Neolithic pottery and animal bone were recovered by the Cunningtons, indicating that the site saw some degree of occupation activity [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112375", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112376", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167470", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Thesanctuary.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/Thesanctuary.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fe7c453a-cabe-4fb2-9009-2ed71a7632fd'::uuid, 'total_power', '28', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:fe7c453a-cabe-4fb2-9009-2ed71a7632fd:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: total_power ''27'' restored on The Sanctuary', 'e3e96861b574aea9a6f2671377c91e32', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 28 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 7 + 5 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:81526", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2035:chunk-0001: description \"The Sanctuary was a late Neolithic stone and timber ceremonial monument on Overton Hill near Avebury, built around 2500 BC. Maud Cunnington''s 1930 excavation revealed two concentric stone circles and five timber post rings, with the outermost stone circle measuring 138 feet in diameter. The site yielded flint tools, animal bones, human remains, and pottery, suggesting ritualistic use. Connected to Avebury''s henge via the 1.5-mile West Kennet Avenue, it is now part of the Stonehenge and Avebury UNESCO World Heritage Site.\" -> ''The Sanctuary was a stone and timber circle near the village of Avebury in the south-western English county of Wiltshire [1]. The ring was part of a tradition of stone circle construction that spread throughout much of Britain, Ireland, and Brittany during the Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age, over a period between 3300 and 900 BCE [1]. The Sanctuary was erected on Overton Hill [1]. It was connected to the Late Neolithic henge and stone circle at Avebury via the West Kennet Avenue of stones [1]. In the early 18th century, the site was recorded by the antiquarian William Stukeley although the stones were destroyed by local farmers in the 1720s [1]. The Sanctuary underwent archaeological excavation by Maud and Ben Cunnington in 1930 [1]. The first stage of activity at the site, some time around 3000 BCE, consisted of a ring of eight wooden posts 4.5 metres across, with a central post, presumed to be a round hut [1]. Neolithic pottery and animal bone were recovered by the Cunningtons, indicating that the site saw some degree of occupation activity [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112375", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112376", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167470", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Thesanctuary.jpg'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:320084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-016: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fe7c453a/Thesanctuary.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('fead42ae-fdd5-48d1-bcc9-567c1c7873b8'::uuid, 'empire_count', '1', '0', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:fead42ae-fdd5-48d1-bcc9-567c1c7873b8:empire_count', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: empire_count ''0'' restored on Denbury Hill', '8081e8061f2944d031cdd7129759a690', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "empire_count = 1 from tag_site(50.500143283339376, -3.6498927144803868, period_start None)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:88688", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2208:chunk-0001: description ''Denbury Hill (also Denbury Camp or Denbury Down) is an Iron Age hillfort near Denbury village in Devon, England, occupying the entire hilltop at 160 metres above sea level. The oval enclosure is defined by a single rampart and ditch with a counterscarp bank, with high embankments on the south and east sides, and two large burial mounds in the centre. The name derives from Old English \"Defna burg\" meaning \"Fort of the men of Devon\". The fort was likely reoccupied after the Roman departure, possibly as a British resistance centre against the Saxons, though this remains uncertain as the site has never been excavated.'' -> ''Denbury Hill (also known as Denbury Camp and Denbury Down) is the name of an Iron Age hill fort near the village of Denbury in Devon, England [1]. The fort is less than a kilometre south west of the village, occupying the entire hilltop of Denbury Down at 160 metres above sea level [1]. It is surrounded on the south and east sides by high embankments [1]. In the centre of the enclosure there are two large burial mounds [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112382", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112383", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: period_start ''-1000'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:167477", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-035: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fead42ae/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fead42ae/East-Facing_View_of_Denbury_Down.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255115", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_name None -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:255116", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-04_fields-wd3-s022: period_start None -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:333224", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-006-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/fead42ae/East-Facing_View_of_Denbury_Down.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/fead42ae/Denbury Hill Fort - Earthworks - geograph.org.uk - 2350383.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387775", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''Undated''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:387776", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-07_mechanical-period-fix-s005: period_start ''-800'' -> None"}]'::jsonb),
