    ('ffd325ad-d579-4510-90c7-acb87b753c88'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-11-07-rollback:ffd325ad-d579-4510-90c7-acb87b753c88:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-07: mystery ''5'' restored on Majorville Cairn and Medicine Wheel', '28baae81e2b9a5d194cc91be9f45a8bc', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Cairn'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 20 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d17040beec27c2c0 (2026-11-07 10:00:39.847916+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 900 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59700 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-05''s own export (2026-11-05 22:52:16.465314+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43311", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0071:chunk-0001: description ''The Majorville Cairn and Medicine Wheel (Iniskim Umaapi) is a sacred site of the Blackfoot Nation located south of Bassano, Alberta. Dated to approximately 3200 BC through artifact stratification, it is one of the oldest religious monuments in the world. The structure consists of a 9 m diameter stone cairn surrounded by a 27 m cobble circle connected by 28 stone spokes, a rare Subgroup 6 configuration shared by only two other known medicine wheels. Projectile points indicate continuous use spanning 4,500 years, with offerings of sweetgrass, sage, tobacco, and cloth still left at the site today.'' -> ''The Majorville Cairn and Medicine Wheel is an archaeological site of the Blackfoot Nation located south of Bassano, Alberta [1]. The medicine wheel sits on top of a grassy hill at an elevation of 918 m overlooking a large area of undisturbed prairie around the Bow River [1]. The structure consists of a round stone cairn, 9 m in diameter, surrounded by a 27 m wide cobble circle connected to the cairn by 28 stone spokes [1]. This arrangement is categorized as Subgroup 6 and of the total 67 known medicine wheels only 3 belong to this category [1]. The site was built in layers, with the earliest C14 date from the cairn determined at 3845 ± 85 radiocarbon years before present [1]. Rocks and arrowheads were added to it until contact with Europeans, although there was a gap in its use between 3000 and 2000 years ago [1]. The site further yielded iniskim stones (\"buffalo calling stones\"), fragments of ammonite fossils that can be found in the bedrock exposed by the Bow River to the east of the site [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:112404", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26d_fields-wd1-s023: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Cairn''"}]'::jsonb),
